{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Header
  Phase 4. Lives in the header section group.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md section 19 and PHASE-2-DESIGN-TOKENS.css for
    heights, logo sizing, spacing, states and targets.
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md for the defects this section must not reproduce.
  - PHASE-3-ASSET-SYSTEM.md for the icon set and the logo position.

  Four Phase 1 findings are answered structurally rather than by styling:

  NAV-01  Below 900px the prototype had no navigation at all: the links were
          hidden and the hamburger was an inert span. Here the menu is a real
          <button> controlling a real panel, with aria-expanded, Escape, focus
          return and a focus trap.
  A11Y-01 Search, account and cart were bare <img> with tabIndex -1. Here they
          are a <button> and two <a> elements, each with an accessible name and
          a target of at least --target-min (44px).
  A11Y-03 Three nav links measured 2.4-2.9:1 over the hero sky. When the header
          overlays a section, --scrim-header is applied unconditionally. The
          scrim is not optional, because the contrast failure was not optional.
  NAV-06  The active state was a hard-coded gold underline on Home. Here it
          comes from link.active and carries aria-current="page".

  Navigation destinations are NOT hard-coded. Phase 1 Appendix A records every
  destination as BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED; a linklist resolves that
  correctly, because the merchant sets the menu in the admin.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign menu = section.settings.menu
  assign has_menu = false
  if menu != blank and linklists[menu].links.size > 0
    assign has_menu = true
  endif

  comment
    THE FALLBACK NAV, USED ONLY WHEN NO MENU IS ASSIGNED.

    Navigation is merchant data: the handle is pinned in header-group.json and
    the links are built in Shopify admin under Content > Menus. Until that menu
    exists, linklists[menu] is empty, has_menu is false, and the header used to
    render no nav at all — on a fresh store that reads as a broken theme rather
    than as an unconfigured one.

    So the design's own four items stand in. A real menu always wins: every
    consumer below branches on has_menu and reaches this only in the else.

    EVERY URL HERE RESOLVES TO A TEMPLATE THIS THEME SHIPS. That is the whole
    constraint, and it is why Shop points at routes.all_products_collection_url
    (/collections/all, which renders collection.json) and NOT at /collections,
    which is the collection LIST route and needs a list-collections template
    this theme does not have. The two are different routes and conflating them
    serves Shopify's error page. The anchors are real: templates/index.json
    sets anchor_id "story" and "verse" on the sections that carry them.
  endcomment
  assign fb_urls = routes.root_url
  assign fb_urls = fb_urls | append: '|' | append: routes.all_products_collection_url
  assign fb_urls = fb_urls | append: '|' | append: routes.root_url | append: '#story'
  assign fb_urls = fb_urls | append: '|' | append: routes.root_url | append: '#verse'
  assign fb_urls = fb_urls | split: '|'

  comment
    Each label is translated into its own variable first. Chaining | t onto an
    append would hand the filter the whole concatenated string instead of a
    single key, and the lookup would miss.
  endcomment
  assign t_home = 'header.fallback_nav.home' | t
  assign t_shop = 'header.fallback_nav.shop' | t
  assign t_story = 'header.fallback_nav.story' | t
  assign t_verse = 'header.fallback_nav.verse' | t

  assign fb_titles = t_home
  assign fb_titles = fb_titles | append: '|' | append: t_shop
  assign fb_titles = fb_titles | append: '|' | append: t_story
  assign fb_titles = fb_titles | append: '|' | append: t_verse
  assign fb_titles = fb_titles | split: '|'

  assign overlay = false
  if section.settings.overlay_first_section and template.name == 'index'
    assign overlay = true
  endif

  assign header_classes = 'header surface-dark'
  if section.settings.sticky
    assign header_classes = header_classes | append: ' header--sticky'
  endif
  if overlay
    assign header_classes = header_classes | append: ' header--overlay'
  endif
-%}

{%- if overlay -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The header publishes the clearance it needs so the section beneath it can
    keep content clear of the navigation without knowing anything about the
    header's settings. Consumed by sections/hero.liquid via
    var(--header-overlay-offset, 0px), which resolves to zero when the header
    sits in normal flow.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <style>
    :root { --header-overlay-offset: var(--header-height-mobile); }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      :root { --header-overlay-offset: var(--header-height-desktop); }
    }
  </style>
{%- endif -%}

<header
  class="{{ header_classes }}"
  data-header
  {% if overlay %}data-header-overlay{% endif %}
>
  {%- if overlay -%}
    {%- comment -%} Mandatory backing. See A11Y-03 above. {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="header__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="header__inner container">
    {%- comment -%} Left: menu button on small screens {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="header__cluster header__cluster--start">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="header__control header__menu-toggle"
        aria-expanded="false"
        aria-controls="MobileMenu"
        data-menu-toggle
      >
        {% render 'icon-menu' %}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'header.menu' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%} Centre-left: the wordmark {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="header__branding">
      {%- if settings.logo != blank -%}
        <a
          class="header__logo-link"
          href="{{ routes.root_url }}"
          {% if template.name == 'index' %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
        >
          {%- liquid
            assign logo_alt = settings.logo.alt | default: shop.name
            assign logo_h_desktop = section.settings.logo_height_desktop
            assign logo_h_mobile = section.settings.logo_height_mobile
            comment
              Built with append, not with "#{...}". Shopify's Liquid does not
              interpolate inside a string literal — the documented way to build
              one is the append filter — so the previous form emitted the
              literal text "#{logo_h_desktop}px" into the style attribute.

              That is not a harmless no-op. A custom property accepts almost
              any token sequence, so the declaration was VALID with a garbage
              value, and being set on the img it shadowed the good :root
              defaults in design-tokens.css. header.css then resolved
              height: var(--logo-height-mobile) to that garbage, which is
              invalid at computed-value time, so height fell back to its
              initial value auto and the logo drew at its intrinsic size.

              It only bit once a logo was uploaded: with the setting empty the
              section takes the text-wordmark branch below, which is why every
              fixture in Phases 4-9 rendered correctly.
            endcomment
            assign logo_style = '--logo-height-desktop: ' | append: logo_h_desktop | append: 'px; --logo-height-mobile: ' | append: logo_h_mobile | append: 'px;'
          -%}
          {{
            settings.logo
            | image_url: width: 600
            | image_tag:
              class: 'header__logo',
              alt: logo_alt,
              widths: '120, 180, 240, 360, 480, 600',
              sizes: '(min-width: 1024px) 240px, 160px',
              preload: true,
              style: logo_style
          }}
        </a>
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          No logo uploaded. The bundled wordmark stands in, so the header reads
          as the brand rather than as plain text; uploading one in Theme settings
          > Brand takes the branch above. The style attribute is built the same
          way and for the same reason documented there.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign logo_h_desktop = section.settings.logo_height_desktop
          assign logo_h_mobile = section.settings.logo_height_mobile
          assign logo_style = '--logo-height-desktop: ' | append: logo_h_desktop | append: 'px; --logo-height-mobile: ' | append: logo_h_mobile | append: 'px;'
        -%}
        <a
          class="header__logo-link"
          href="{{ routes.root_url }}"
          {% if template.name == 'index' %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
        >
          <img
            class="header__logo"
            src="{{ 'logo.png' | asset_url }}"
            width="500"
            height="500"
            sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 240px, 160px"
            style="{{ logo_style }}"
            alt="{{ shop.name | escape }}"
          >
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%} Centre: primary navigation, desktop only {%- endcomment -%}
    <nav class="header__nav" aria-label="{{ 'header.primary_nav' | t }}">
      <ul class="header__nav-list" role="list">
        {%- if has_menu -%}
          {%- for link in linklists[menu].links -%}
            <li class="header__nav-item">
              <a
                class="header__nav-link{% if link.active %} is-active{% endif %}"
                href="{{ link.url }}"
                {% if link.active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
              >{{ link.title }}</a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- for title in fb_titles -%}
            <li class="header__nav-item">
              <a class="header__nav-link" href="{{ fb_urls[forloop.index0] }}">{{ title }}</a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    {%- comment -%} Right: utility navigation {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="header__cluster header__cluster--end">
      {%- if section.settings.show_search -%}
        <a class="header__control" href="{{ routes.search_url }}" data-search-trigger>
          {% render 'icon-search' %}
          <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'header.search' | t }}</span>
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        PHASE 15 REPLACED THIS CONTROL AND NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE.

        It was an <a href="{{ routes.account_url }}"> carrying the Phase 3
        account icon and a label that switched between Account and Log in. It
        worked, and Shopify still redirects that URL. It is gone anyway,
        because the platform moved underneath it.

        WHY A SHOPIFY-OWNED ELEMENT IS HERE AT ALL.
        Legacy customer accounts were deprecated on 2026-02-26 and the account
        experience now lives on Shopify's own host, off this origin. A link
        could only ever navigate away from the store; <shopify-account> opens
        the account sheet in place. On a store still using legacy accounts
        Shopify documents the component as degrading to exactly what this used
        to be — a link straight to the sign-in page — so the replacement loses
        nothing on either system.

        ONE ENTRY POINT, NOT TWO.
        The old link is not kept beside this. Two account controls on one
        header would behave differently from each other — one opening a sheet,
        one navigating off-site — and there is no width in the 375px end
        cluster for a second control anyway.

        WHAT THIS THEME DOES NOT CONTROL.
        Shopify owns this element and says it may change it independently of
        the theme. Only three things are contractual: the published
        --shopify-account-* custom properties, ::part(signed-out-avatar), and
        the signed-out-avatar slot. assets/header.css uses the first and this
        uses the third; nothing here reaches inside it with a descendant
        selector, because that would break the first time Shopify ships a
        change.

        THE SLOT KEEPS THE BRAND'S OWN ICON.
        Without it the header would show Shopify's default avatar in place of
        the account mark Phase 3 drew to the Phase 2 icon contract. The slot is
        the documented way to supply your own, so the icon survives the change.

        THE HIDDEN LABEL IS DELIBERATE BELT AND BRACES.
        Dawn ships this component with no label at all, which implies Shopify
        names the control itself. "Implies" is not good enough for a Level A
        requirement that cannot be tested from here, so a visually-hidden name
        goes in the slot. If Shopify sets its own aria-label that label wins
        and this is ignored; if it does not, the control is still named. The
        two outcomes are "correct" and "correct" — whereas omitting it has an
        outcome that is an unnamed control (SC 4.1.2).
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if shop.customer_accounts_enabled and section.settings.show_account -%}
        <shopify-account
          class="header__control header__account"
          menu="{{ settings.customer_account_menu | default: 'customer-account-main-menu' }}"
        >
          <span class="header__account-avatar" slot="signed-out-avatar">
            {% render 'icon-account' %}
            <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'header.account' | t }}</span>
          </span>
        </shopify-account>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        PHASE 8 TOUCHED THIS ELEMENT AND NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE.

        It is still the same link to the cart page it was in Phase 4, and with
        scripting off that is exactly what it does. assets/cart.js cancels the
        navigation and opens the drawer instead — and leaves a modified click
        alone, so cmd-click still opens the cart page in a new tab.

        Its contents moved into a snippet because the Section Rendering API
        needs a second copy of this markup to swap in after a cart change, and
        two hand-maintained copies drift. The count is still cart.item_count
        and is still absent when the cart is empty (NAV-09: the prototype's
        badge was the literal character 0).
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        aria-controls names the drawer, so it is only truthful while a drawer
        exists. With the cart set to "Cart page" the layout renders no drawer
        and this stays what it already is without scripting: a link to the cart.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        PHASE 16: aria-controls carries a SECOND condition now. The drawer is
        absent for two reasons and this only knew about one — layout/theme.liquid
        skips it when the merchant picks the cart page, and ALSO on the cart
        template itself, because there the page is the cart. Without the second
        arm this control pointed aria-controls at an element id that does not
        exist on /cart.

        The reasoning lives out here rather than among the attributes. A
        comment tag with whitespace control, placed INSIDE an element tag, eats
        the space before it: the first attempt at this fix shipped
        data-cart-bubble and aria-controls fused into one attribute, which
        removed the hook the entire cart script binds to. Phase 13 recorded the
        neighbouring hazard, that comment prose containing tag syntax closes the
        tag that encloses it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if section.settings.show_cart -%}
        <a
          class="header__control header__cart"
          href="{{ routes.cart_url }}"
          data-cart-bubble
          {% if settings.cart_type != 'page' and template.name != 'cart' %}aria-controls="CartDrawer"{% endif %}
        >
          {% render 'cart-icon-bubble' %}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Phase 13. The search panel.

    The trigger above is still a plain link to routes.search_url and is left
    byte-identical on purpose: with no script it navigates to a complete search
    page, and a <button> with no script would be a control that does nothing.
    assets/header.js intercepts the click and opens this instead, so the panel
    is an enhancement over a working link rather than a replacement for one.

    An overlay panel rather than an expanding field, and that is arithmetic. At
    375px the header row has 327px of content box; the start cluster takes 44,
    the end cluster 148 and the two grid gaps 32, leaving the branding track
    103px. A usable field needs about 180. Taking it means the logo goes or the
    bar reflows — which is redesigning the header, not preserving it. A layer
    adds nothing to that row.

    aria-haspopup and aria-expanded are NOT printed here. They would be a claim
    about behaviour that only exists once the script runs; header.js applies
    them at upgrade.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="header__search" id="HeaderSearch" data-search-panel hidden>
    <form
      class="header__search-form"
      action="{{ routes.search_url }}"
      method="get"
      role="search"
    >
      <label class="visually-hidden" for="HeaderSearchInput">
        {{ 'header.search_label' | t }}
      </label>
      <input
        class="header__search-input"
        id="HeaderSearchInput"
        type="search"
        name="q"
        placeholder="{{ 'header.search_placeholder' | t }}"
        data-search-input
      >
      {%- comment -%}
        type=product, matching what templates/search.json configures, so the
        header and the results page agree on what is being searched.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="type" value="product">
      <button type="submit" class="button button--primary header__search-submit">
        {{ 'header.search' | t }}
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="header__control" data-search-close>
        {% render 'icon-close' %}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'header.search_close' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} Mobile menu panel {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="header__panel" id="MobileMenu" data-menu-panel hidden>
    <div class="header__panel-header">
      <span class="header__panel-title">{{ 'header.menu' | t }}</span>
      <button type="button" class="header__control" data-menu-close>
        {% render 'icon-close' %}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'header.close' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <nav class="header__panel-nav" aria-label="{{ 'header.mobile_nav' | t }}">
      <ul class="header__panel-list" role="list">
        {%- if has_menu -%}
          {%- for link in linklists[menu].links -%}
            <li>
              <a
                class="header__panel-link{% if link.active %} is-active{% endif %}"
                href="{{ link.url }}"
                {% if link.active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
              >{{ link.title }}</a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- for title in fb_titles -%}
            <li>
              <a class="header__panel-link" href="{{ fb_urls[forloop.index0] }}">{{ title }}</a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    {%- comment -%}
      The no_menu notice now shows only in the Theme Editor. On the storefront
      the fallback nav above stands in, so telling a shopper the merchant has
      not picked a menu would be addressing the wrong person; in the editor the
      merchant IS the reader, and the cue to build a real menu still matters.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if has_menu == false and request.design_mode -%}
      <p class="header__panel-empty">
        {{ 'header.no_menu' | t }}
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="header__overlay" data-menu-overlay hidden></div>
</header>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Header",
  "tag": "div",
  "class": "header-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Branding"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The logo is uploaded once in Theme settings > Brand, so the header and the footer always show the same mark. Its size on each is set here."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "logo_height_desktop",
      "label": "Logo height, desktop",
      "min": 40,
      "max": 120,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 78
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "logo_height_mobile",
      "label": "Logo height, mobile",
      "min": 32,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 56
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Navigation"
    },
    {
      "type": "link_list",
      "id": "menu",
      "label": "Menu",
      "default": "main-menu",
      "info": "Destinations are set on the menu in Navigation, not in the theme."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Utility"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_search",
      "label": "Show search",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_account",
      "label": "Show account",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Only appears when customer accounts are enabled for the store, under Settings > Customer accounts. The account pages themselves are hosted by Shopify and are styled in the checkout and accounts editor, not in this theme."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_cart",
      "label": "Show cart",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Leave this on unless you have another route to the cart. With it off the header has no cart control at all, and a customer who has added something can only reach the cart by typing the address."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Behaviour"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "sticky",
      "label": "Stick to the top on scroll",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "overlay_first_section",
      "label": "Overlay the first section on the home page",
      "default": true,
      "info": "Matches the approved design, where the header sits over the hero. A dark scrim is always applied behind the header when this is on: without it three of the five links measured 2.4-2.9:1 against the sky, below the 4.5:1 minimum."
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": {
    "groups": [
      "header"
    ]
  }
}
{% endschema %}
